Third-party origins
===================

pyxdw/lzh.py (LH4/LH5-style static-Huffman LZH codec)

  Parts of this file correspond closely in structure to ar002 by
  Haruhiko Okumura and may be adapted from it:

    - _tokenize_lzss: the percolating-update Patricia-tree matcher of
      ar002 encode.c
    - _huffman_lengths (heap construction, leaf ordering and length
      assignment): ar002 maketree.c
    - _write_auxiliary_tree, _write_main_tree, _write_lzh_block: the
      length-table and block encoding of ar002 huf.c

  The author of ar002 states at the distribution site:

    "ar002 -- written by Haruhiko Okumura and released to the public domain"

    https://okumuralab.org/~okumura/compression/ar002/00README.txt

  The Python code is restructured for Python (for example, token lists
  instead of packed buffers, explicit flag checks, input validation, no
  file, CRC or progress handling). Which copy of ar002 was consulted
  during the original development was not recorded. Source hashes and
  open questions are listed in docs/third_party.md.

  The MIT License above applies to the contributions of the pyxdw
  authors. It does not restrict public-domain material.

  _package_merge_lengths is based on the Package-Merge algorithm
  described in L. L. Larmore and D. S. Hirschberg, "A Fast Algorithm for
  Optimal Length-Limited Huffman Codes", Journal of the ACM 37(3), 1990,
  pp. 464-473. Its design and test records are described in
  docs/third_party.md.

  The decoder follows the LH4/LH5 bit-stream format. It decodes with
  canonical-code dictionaries rather than the lookup tables of the C
  implementations.
